    "q-73857": {
      "dom_id": "step-q73857",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "At which points on the surface $z={x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}+6y+10$ is the tangent plane horizontal?",
      "worked_solution": "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}+6y+10) \\\\  & =2x-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}+6y+10) \\\\  & =2y+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2x-2,\\,2y+6,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}2x-2=0 \\\\ 2y+6=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=1$ while the second equation gives $y=-3.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=1$ and $y=-3$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz={1}^{2}-2(1)+(-3)^{2}+6(-3)+10=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(1,-3,0).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
