    "q-129557": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129557",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "At which point on the surface $z=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v=⟨4,-2,-1⟩?$",
      "worked_solution": "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=6x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨6x,\\,2y,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨4,-2,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨6x,\\,2y,\\,-1⟩=⟨4,-2,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6cx=4 \\\\ 2cy=-2 \\\\ -c=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n6(1)x=4\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n2(1)y=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}+(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(\\frac{2}{3},-1,\\frac{7}{3}).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
